<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shah Shuja Durrani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1785">1785</a>–<a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shah Shuja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> was a ruler of the Durrani Empire, reigning from 1803 to 1809 and again from 1839 to 1842. Driven out by his brother Shah Mahmud and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai</a> chiefs, he lived for decades in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. The British restored him to the throne in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-afghan-war">First Anglo-Afghan War</a>, but he was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> in 1842 after their retreat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-shuja-durrani.jpg" alt="Shah Shuja Durrani" width="320" height="498" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Print made by: Lowes Cato Dickinson After: Sir Vincent Eyre · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lithograph_of_Shah_Shujah_in_1843.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shuja Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> was born on 4 November 1785, a son of Timur Shah Durrani and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozai</a> lineage of the Durrani clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtuns</a>. He was governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> from 1798 to 1801. In October 1801 he proclaimed himself king after the deposition of his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaman-shah-durrani">Zaman Shah</a>, whom his step-brother Mahmud Shah had blinded, since by tradition a blind man cannot be ruler. He ascended the throne properly on 13 July 1803.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After coming to power in 1803 Shuja ended the blood feud with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai</a> family, forgave them and married their sister Wafa Begum to make an alliance. In 1809 he allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> as a defence against a feared invasion, and a British mission under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mountstuart-elphinstone">Mountstuart Elphinstone</a> visited him. In June 1809 Mahmud Shah overthrew him at the battle of Nimla. He was captured in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attock">Attock</a> in 1811 and 1812, taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and then went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> took from him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koh-i-noor">Koh-i-Noor</a> and other diamonds. He escaped to Ludhiana and from 1818 drew a pension from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>.</p><p>In exile the American Josiah Harlan, who visited his court, recorded that Shuja had the courtiers and slaves who displeased him mutilated, and he later judged him a tyrant. In 1833 Shuja agreed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> to march through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and cede <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> if they took it, but the following year he was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>, in July, by the Qandahar Sardars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan</a>. In 1838 he won the support of the British and Ranjit Singh. The British restored him on 7 August 1839, thirty years after his deposition. By then most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>, it is said, remembered him as cruel, and he shut himself in the Bala Hissar and took revenge on those he felt had betrayed him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Under religious pressure Shuja mustered an army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> on 4 April 1842, ostensibly to attack the British. The next day his godson Shujah ud-Daula, who had been passed over for a command, led a gang that ambushed and killed him. He died on 5 April 1842.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shuja Shah Durrani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.62,65.72&amp;year=1822" aria-label="Kandahar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Shah Shuja Durrani"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5416 4851h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5416 4851h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.62,65.72&amp;year=1822">What was here in 1822? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
